# 127.0.0.1, not localhost: on Windows, localhost resolves to IPv6 first and
# Postgres in Docker listens on the IPv4 loopback, so every new connection pays
# about two seconds -- longer than a Prisma transaction will wait, which fails
# the billing tests with a transaction timeout that looks nothing like a DNS
# problem.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://curios:curios@127.0.0.1:5452/curios
DATABASE_URL_READONLY=postgresql://curios_readonly:curios_readonly@127.0.0.1:5452/curios
DATABASE_URL_TEST=postgresql://curios:curios@127.0.0.1:5453/curios_test
DATABASE_URL_TEST_READONLY=postgresql://curios_readonly:curios_readonly@127.0.0.1:5453/curios_test
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6399
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=replace-this-with-your-own-anthropic-api-key

# Embeddings, for retrieval. Every fact stores the question it answers; that
# question is embedded once, the visitor's question is embedded per ask, and
# the closest few facts are what the model sees. Without this key the API and
# the workers refuse to start -- deliberately, because the alternative failure
# is invisible: every page would answer from every fact, at roughly twice the
# cost, and look completely healthy doing it. See devDocs/slice-12-build-spec.md.
OPENAI_API_KEY=replace-this-with-your-own-openai-api-key

GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI=http://localhost:3001/auth/google/callback
SESSION_SECRET=replace-this-generate-your-own-with-openssl-rand-hex-32
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
NEXT_PUBLIC_API_URL=http://localhost:3001

# Rate limiting. These exist to bound spend: /chat/:handle is anonymous by
# design and can never sit behind a session cookie, and every call to it or to
# /ingest spends model tokens. The values below are the defaults -- set them
# only to override. Limits are enforced in Redis and fail closed, so if Redis
# is unreachable the paid routes refuse rather than serve unmetered.
RATE_LIMIT_CHAT_PER_PAGE=30
RATE_LIMIT_CHAT_PER_IP=100
RATE_LIMIT_INGEST_PER_IP=5
RATE_LIMIT_INGEST_PER_PAGE=20
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_IP=20
RATE_LIMIT_AUTH_PER_EMAIL=10

# The conversation cap. Each page may start this many NEW conversations per
# calendar month before its chat degrades to a fixed reply -- see
# packages/core/src/cap.ts. This is not a per-message spend limit; spend
# within a conversation is bounded separately by the RATE_LIMIT_CHAT_* values
# above. Applies to every page alike -- there is no per-page override yet.
# Read by the API when a conversation starts and by the web app when it renders
# /app/billing, so give both the same value or leave it unset in both. A screen
# showing a cap the API does not enforce is worse than a screen showing none.
# The value below is the default (200, roughly seven conversations a day);
# set this only to override it.
CONVERSATION_CAP_PER_MONTH=200

# --- The crawler's address guard ------------------------------------------
# Ingest refuses to fetch private, loopback, link-local and metadata addresses,
# in the API (what may be enqueued) and in the worker's connector (what may
# open a socket). See devDocs/ssrf-ingest-guard-design.md.
#
# This switch turns that off. It exists for ONE reason: the manual ingest
# walkthrough points the crawler at a static page on 127.0.0.1, and a guard
# that makes the verification path impossible is a guard someone comments out.
#
# Anything other than the exact string "true" denies -- TRUE, 1 and yes all
# mean no -- so a typo fails closed. Never set this in production.
INGEST_ALLOW_PRIVATE_ADDRESSES=true

# --- Rate limits in development -----------------------------------------
# The defaults above are the production values, and they are tight: ingest is
# 5 per HOUR per address, auth is 20 per 15 minutes. Clicking through
# onboarding a few times will hit them.
#
# Raise them in your own .env. Do NOT comment the limiters out. Four real
# limiter bugs were found during slice 2 precisely because the limiters were
# live in the environment the work happened in -- /auth/me sitting on a
# credential budget, the per-email limiter halving the budget on bodyless
# routes, unauthenticated callers burning an owner's ingest budget, and Redis
# counts bleeding between test runs. Disabling them locally hides that whole
# class of bug until production. It also breaks the dev-and-production-are-
# the-same-path property that Mailpit exists to preserve, and it hides a dead
# Redis, since the limiters fail closed.
#
# Raise the two groups differently, because they guard different things:
#
#   AUTH_*   guards against credential stuffing. Locally that guards against
#            nobody, so raise it hard (1000 is fine).
#   INGEST_* and CHAT_* are WALLET controls. Every ingest spends Opus tokens
#            over a whole crawled site; every chat spends Haiku. Raise these
#            only enough for a day's clicking -- 50 -- so a runaway loop still
#            stops itself before it bills you.
#
# The test suite does not read these. apps/api/vitest.setup.ts pins the
# production values, so a suite result never depends on what is in your .env.
# -------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Prefix for the limiters' Redis keys.
# Two deployments pointed at one Redis
# share buckets unless they set different values here.
RATE_LIMIT_NAMESPACE=curios-rl:

# Blank trusts nothing. Rate limits key off the caller address, so behind a
# load balancer this MUST be set or every visitor is throttled as one caller.
# Prefer the proxy address or subnet (comma separated) over "true": with
# "true" and any path that bypasses the proxy, a caller can spoof
# X-Forwarded-For and hand itself an unlimited budget.
TRUST_PROXY=

# Mail. Local development sends to Mailpit, which needs no account and no
# credentials -- read what was sent at http://localhost:8075. Production points
# SMTP_* at a hosted sender; nothing outside lib/mailer.ts knows which is in use.
SMTP_HOST=localhost
SMTP_PORT=1075
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASSWORD=
MAIL_FROM=Curios <hello@curios.my>

# Blank in development, where the web and API share localhost and cookies
# ignore port. In production set .curios.my so the two origins share the
# session cookie.
COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# --- Billplz -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Payments. Sandbox by default: a sandbox account has its own keys and its own
# domain, and takes simulated FPX payments with test bank credentials -- no real
# money, no business registration. Production points BILLPLZ_BASE_URL at
# https://www.billplz.com with that account's keys. See
# devDocs/slice-9-build-spec.md.
#
# In production the API refuses to start without these, and so do the workers --
# which need the API key and base URL, plus APP_URL above, because the renewal
# reminder links back to /app/billing.
#
# In development they can be left out entirely: delete all six lines and both
# processes start with billing switched off (a warning at startup, and "start a
# payment" answers BILLING_NOT_CONFIGURED). All six or none -- a partial set still
# refuses to start and names the missing one.
#
# The callback cannot reach localhost. Billplz's servers call
# BILLPLZ_CALLBACK_URL, and a laptop is not on the internet, so in development
# the callback never arrives. The redirect still grants a payment -- the browser
# follows it -- and so does the workers' hourly pending check. To exercise the
# callback itself, run a tunnel and point BILLPLZ_CALLBACK_URL at it; see
# devDocs/manual-verification-billing.md.
BILLPLZ_API_KEY=replace-this-with-your-billplz-sandbox-secret-key
BILLPLZ_COLLECTION_ID=replace-this-with-your-sandbox-collection-id
BILLPLZ_X_SIGNATURE_KEY=replace-this-with-your-sandbox-x-signature-key
BILLPLZ_BASE_URL=https://www.billplz-sandbox.com
BILLPLZ_CALLBACK_URL=http://localhost:3001/billing/billplz/callback
BILLPLZ_REDIRECT_URL=http://localhost:3001/billing/billplz/return

# Starting a payment raises a bill at Billplz, so it is limited per account. The
# value below is the default; apps/api/vitest.setup.ts pins it for the tests.
RATE_LIMIT_BILLING_PER_ACCOUNT=10
